</div>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><math id="footnotex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\star" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\star</annotation></semantics></math> Equal contribution.</span></span></span>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods have become the standard paradigm for adapting large-scale models. Among these techniques, Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation (DoRA) has been shown to improve both the learning capacity and training stability of the vanilla Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) method by explicitly decomposing pre-trained weights into magnitude and directional components. In this work, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DoRAN</span>, a new variant of DoRA designed to further stabilize training and boost the sample efficiency of DoRA. Our approach includes two key stages: <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i)</span> injecting noise into the denominator of DoRA’s weight decomposition, which serves as an adaptive regularizer to mitigate instabilities; and <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii)</span> replacing static low-rank matrices with auxiliary networks that generate them dynamically, enabling parameter coupling across layers and yielding better sample efficiency in both theory and practice. Comprehensive experiments on vision and language benchmarks show that DoRAN consistently outperforms LoRA, DoRA, and other PEFT baselines. These results underscore the effectiveness of combining stabilization through noise-based regularization with network-based parameter generation, offering a promising direction for robust and efficient fine-tuning of foundation models.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid growth of large-scale pre-trained models has reshaped modern machine learning, enabling state-of-the-art performance across a wide range of vision and language tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. The prohibitive cost of fully fine-tuning these models has spurred significant interest in parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods, which achieve strong downstream performance while modifying only a small fraction of parameters. A variety of PEFT methods have been developed to adapt large-scale pre-trained models without incurring the cost of full fine-tuning. Early approaches, such as adapter layers, insert small trainable modules between frozen transformer blocks, while prefix- and prompt-tuning prepend learnable vectors to the model’s input space. More recently, Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) has become a foundational technique for efficient fine-tuning of large-scale models across multiple AI domains. Originally proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, LoRA introduces low-rank trainable adapters into existing weight matrices, reducing computational overhead while maintaining high task performance. Beyond its success in natural language processing (NLP), LoRA has demonstrated strong adaptability in computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, speech processing, and reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. LoRA also plays a pivotal role in federated and distributed learning, enabling efficient personalization while preserving data privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite its effectiveness in providing a flexible low-rank adaptation, LoRA’s rigid low-rank formulation can limit both representational capacity and training stability. Several extensions have sought to address these shortcomings. For instances, Dynamic LoRA (DyLoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> and AdaLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> proposes rank scheduling during training to allocate variable ranks dynamically, leading to more expressive feature subspaces. Notably, DoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> introduces a directional-based normalization approach: instead of learning flexible low-rank updates directly, it decomposes pre-trained weights into magnitude and directional components. This design improves representational power relative to vanilla LoRA and mitigates some instability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While DoRA represents a significant advance, it still inherits two challenges. First, DoRA relies on strict normalization, which makes it sensitive to optimization instabilities: when the adapted weight norm approaches zero, gradients can explode, destabilizing training. Second, DoRA still employs static low-rank matrices that are optimized independently at each layer, underutilizing cross-layer correlations and often leading to redundant or poorly coordinated updates. These challenges motivate the following central question of our work:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="107.9" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 613.78 107.9" width="613.78"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,107.9) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 102 C 0 105.26 2.64 107.9 5.91 107.9 L 607.87 107.9 C 611.14 107.9 613.78 105.26 613.78 102 L 613.78 5.91 C 613.78 2.64 611.14 0 607.87 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 102 C 1.97 104.17 3.73 105.93 5.91 105.93 L 607.87 105.93 C 610.05 105.93 611.81 104.17 611.81 102 L 611.81 5.91 C 611.81 3.73 610.05 1.97 607.87 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 17.72 75.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:41.8em;--ltx-fo-height:1.95em;--ltx-fo-depth:4.99em;font-size:10pt;" height="96.09" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.98)" width="578.39"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:41.8em;">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Q)</span><span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> <span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we unify the strengths of <span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">direction-based normalization</span> and <span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">flexible low-rank adaptation</span> into a single framework that is both stable and expressive?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address this question, we propose Weight-<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>ecomposed L<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o</span>w-<span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>ank <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>daptation with <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">N</span>oise injection (DoRAN), a PEFT approach that simultaneously <span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">stabilizes optimization</span> and <span id="S1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">enriches low-rank representations</span>. DoRAN introduces two key components. <span id="S1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i) Stabilization via learnable noise:</span> We augment DoRA’s normalization with a learnable offset in the denominator, which functions as an adaptive regularizer. This simple yet powerful modification eliminates singularities at small norms, ensures gradients remain well-conditioned, and allows the model to smoothly interpolate between two regimes: purely directional updates (as in DoRA) and linear scaling (as in LoRA). In this way, DoRAN adaptively preserves magnitude information of the gradient signal instead of discarding it entirely like DoRA, as we will reveal in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Gradient Analysis ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, leading to more expressive and stable updates. <span id="S1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii) Dynamic generation of low-rank factors:</span> Instead of directly learning a separate low-rank adapter at each layer, we dynamically generate the adapters through a hypernetwork with a shared backbone and head-specific output heads. This coupling encourages cross-layer information sharing, enforces structural consistency across the model, and improves sample efficiency by leveraging shared latent structure. This design is motivated by recent studies which demonstrated that hypernetworks enhances generalization and stability by enabling adaptive weight generation conditioned on task representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Besides, this design also provides a richer inductive bias that regularizes the adaptation space and mitigates redundancy across layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, as we will show in our experiments, both components contribute significantly to the performance improvements. Moreover, these two components are synergistic: the learnable noise term ensures that gradient signals remain stable and informative, while the hypernetwork ensures that these stabilized signals are used to generate structured low-rank updates. Together, they provide a unified framework that bridges the gap between direction-based normalization and flexible low-rank adaptation, offering both robustness and expressiveness for fine-tuning large-scale models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We validate DoRAN through extensive experiments on various benchmarks, including vision (VTAB-1K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, FGVC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> with ViT-B/16 architechture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>) and language benchmarks (commonsense reasoning with LLaMA-7B/13B architechture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>). DoRAN consistently outperforms LoRA, DoRA, and other PEFT baselines, while introducing negligible additional overhead. We also provide theoretical and empirical evidence that DoRAN significantly improves sample efficiency by leveraging shared structure across layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p8.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="300.91" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 613.78 300.91" width="613.78"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,300.91) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCCC;" fill="#CCCCCC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 3.46 L 0 297.45 C 0 299.36 1.55 300.91 3.46 300.91 L 610.32 300.91 C 612.23 300.91 613.78 299.36 613.78 297.45 L 613.78 3.46 C 613.78 1.55 612.23 0 610.32 0 L 3.46 0 C 1.55 0 0 1.55 0 3.46 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 3.46 L 0.69 297.45 C 0.69 298.98 1.93 300.22 3.46 300.22 L 610.32 300.22 C 611.85 300.22 613.09 298.98 613.09 297.45 L 613.09 3.46 C 613.09 1.93 611.85 0.69 610.32 0.69 L 3.46 0.69 C 1.93 0.69 0.69 1.93 0.69 3.46 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 12.93 234.25)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:42.49em;--ltx-fo-height:3.88em;--ltx-fo-depth:15.99em;font-size:10pt;" height="275.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 53.73)" width="587.94"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p8.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:42.49em;">
<span id="S1.p8.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Contributions.<span id="S1.p8.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> In summary, our contributions are threefold:</span></span></span>
<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We propose a stabilized weight decomposition with learnable noise that removes the singularity of DoRA and adaptively preserves magnitude information, and a hypernetwork-based reparameterization of low-rank factors, enabling cross-layer parameter coupling and improved sample efficiency.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We provide a theoretical analysis showing that this design significantly enhances the sample efficiency from a </span><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">exponential rate</span><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to a </span><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">polynomial rate</span><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We demonstrate through various experimental settings that DoRAN achieves state-of-the-art parameter efficiency, consistently outperforming LoRA, DoRA, and other PEFT baselines on both vision and language tasks.</span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Together, we show that combining noise-based stabilization with hypernetwork-driven parameter generation offers a robust and generalizable path forward for fine-tuning foundation models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04331v1/doran.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:490/306;" width="490" height="306"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of DoRAN. The matrices <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{1}^{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{1}^{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are shared across query and value projection layers, as well as across all attention heads within the same Transformer block.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation.</span> We denote <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]=\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]=\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> for an integer <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a vector <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will use both notations
<math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=(u^{(1)},u^{(2)},\ldots,u^{(d)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=(u^{(1)},u^{(2)},\ldots,u^{(d)})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=(u_{1},u_{2},\ldots,u_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=(u_{1},u_{2},\ldots,u_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> interchangeably.
Given a multi-index vector <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\ldots,\alpha_{d})\in\mathbb{N}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\ldots,\alpha_{d})\in\mathbb{N}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write
<math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u^{\alpha}=u_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}u_{2}^{\alpha_{2}}\cdots u_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\alpha}=u_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}u_{2}^{\alpha_{2}}\cdots u_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\alpha|=\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\alpha|=\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha!=\alpha_{1}!\alpha_{2}!\cdots\alpha_{d}!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>!</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>!</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>!</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha!=\alpha_{1}!\alpha_{2}!\cdots\alpha_{d}!</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|u\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|u\|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Euclidean norm of <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cardinality of a set <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
For two positive sequences <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{n})_{n\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{n})_{n\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b_{n})_{n\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b_{n})_{n\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}=\mathcal{O}(b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}=\mathcal{O}(b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}\lesssim b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}\lesssim b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a constant <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}\leq Cb_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}\leq Cb_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}=\mathcal{O}_{P}(b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}=\mathcal{O}_{P}(b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}/b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}/b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded in probability.
Next, we use the notation <math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}=\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}=\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> when
<math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}=\mathcal{O}_{P}(b_{n}\log^{c}(b_{n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}=\mathcal{O}_{P}(b_{n}\log^{c}(b_{n}))</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we define the inner product of two matrices as <math id="S2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\bm{A}},{\bm{B}}\rangle=\operatorname{Trace}({\bm{A}}^{\top}{\bm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Trace</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑩</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\bm{A}},{\bm{B}}\rangle=\operatorname{Trace}({\bm{A}}^{\top}{\bm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-head Self-attention (MSA).</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-attention is the central mechanism of the Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. Given an input sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> dimension, the model projects <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}</annotation></semantics></math> into query, key, and value matrices via learned linear transformations <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}=\bm{X}\bm{W}_{Q},\bm{K}=\bm{X}\bm{W}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}=\bm{X}\bm{W}_{Q},\bm{K}=\bm{X}\bm{W}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}=\bm{X}\bm{W}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}=\bm{X}\bm{W}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{Q},\bm{W}_{k},\bm{W}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{Q},\bm{W}_{k},\bm{W}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The attention weight is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Attention}(\bm{Q},\bm{K},\bm{V})=\mathrm{softmax}(\frac{{\bm{Q}}{\bm{K}}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}){\bm{V}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Attention</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑲</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Attention}(\bm{Q},\bm{K},\bm{V})=\mathrm{softmax}(\frac{{\bm{Q}}{\bm{K}}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}){\bm{V}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Multi-head Self-attention (MSA) extends this mechanism by introducing multiple parallel attention operations or "heads", where each attention head processes the input sequence from a different representational subspace. Formally, for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> heads, the output of an MSA layer is calculated as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MSA}(\bm{X}_{Q},\bm{X}_{K},\bm{X}_{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MSA</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MSA}(\bm{X}_{Q},\bm{X}_{K},\bm{X}_{V})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Concat}(\mathrm{head}_{1},\cdots,\mathrm{head}_{h})\bm{W}_{O}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Concat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>head</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>head</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Concat}(\mathrm{head}_{1},\cdots,\mathrm{head}_{h})\bm{W}_{O},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>head</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Attention}(\bm{X}\bm{W}^{(i)}_{Q},\bm{X}\bm{W}^{(i)}_{K},\bm{X}\bm{W}^{(i)}_{V})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Attention</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Attention}(\bm{X}\bm{W}^{(i)}_{Q},\bm{X}\bm{W}^{(i)}_{K},\bm{X}\bm{W}^{(i)}_{V}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA and DoRA.</span> Low-rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> is one of the most widely used PEFT approaches. The key idea is to decompose the weight updates into low-rank matrices and fine-tune these low-rank matrices while keeping the original weights frozen. Formally, given a pre-trained weight matrix <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>, LoRA freezes <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and models the weight update <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\bm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\bm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> as two low-rank matrices <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{W}=\bm{W}_{0}+\Delta\bm{W}=\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{W}=\bm{W}_{0}+\Delta\bm{W}=\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>, with rank <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(d,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(d,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. On performing weight decomposition on fine-tuned weight matrices, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> found that LoRA and full fine-tuning show different learning patterns. To resolve this difference, weight-decomposed low-rank adaptation (DoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> explicitly decomposes the pre-trained weight into its magnitude and directional component and fine-tunes both components. Then, DoRA performs updates as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{W}=m\frac{{\bm{V}}+\Delta{\bm{V}}}{\|{\bm{V}}+\Delta{\bm{V}}\|_{c}}=m\frac{\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}}{\|\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}\|_{c}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{W}=m\frac{{\bm{V}}+\Delta{\bm{V}}}{\|{\bm{V}}+\Delta{\bm{V}}\|_{c}}=m\frac{\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}}{\|\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}\|_{c}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B},\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B},\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable low-rank matrices, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable vector, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector-wise norm of
a matrix across each column. In this work, we aim to improve DoRA by introducing noise-stabilized normalization to prevent instability and by utilizing auxiliary networks to generate low-rank updates, thereby enhancing both robustness and parameter efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivations.</span> DoRA enhances stability by decomposing pre-trained weights into magnitude and directional components. Nevertheless, adaptation can still suffer from instability. In particular, when the normalization denominator becomes excessively small, DoRA may encounter gradient explosions, as we will discuss in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Gradient Analysis ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Moreover, standard DoRA employs static, layer-specific low-rank matrices. When each adapter <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{A},\bm{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{A},\bm{B})</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized independently, they risk diverging toward inconsistent or redundant solutions. To address these limitations, we propose Stabilized Weight-<span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>ecomposed L<span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o</span>w-<span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>ank <span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>daptation via <span id="S3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">N</span>oise Injection and Auxiliary Networks (DoRAN), which integrates two complementary components. <span id="S3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span>, DoRAN introduces a learnable stabilization noise term in the normalization step, which provides more stable and expressive updates. <span id="S3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second</span>, instead of directly optimizing the low-rank matrices, DoRAN employs hypernetworks to dynamically generate low-rank matrices.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>DoRAN: DoRA with Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Noise Injection.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first enhance stability by introducing a trainable stabilization noise term <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{W}=\underline{m}\frac{\bm{W}_{0}+\underline{\bm{B}\bm{A}}}{\|\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}\|_{c}+\tau}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{W}=\underline{m}\frac{\bm{W}_{0}+\underline{\bm{B}\bm{A}}}{\|\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}\|_{c}+\tau},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as an adaptive regularizer, ensuring stable normalization. We design <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> as a <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">learnable scalar</span> rather than a fixed constant for a key reasons: As we will reveal in the gradient analysis (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Gradient Analysis ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), the value of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> determines the balance between two adaptation regimes: when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the update reduces to DoRA-style direction learning, while large <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> yields LoRA-like scaling. By making <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> learnable, the model can automatically interpolate between these extremes, adaptively finding the most effective balance between directional learning and stable norm control.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Auxiliary Networks.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the term <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, DoRAN further enhances flexibility by implementing the low-rank matrices <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}</annotation></semantics></math> through auxiliary networks rather than optimizing them directly:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{B}=g_{\bm{B}}(\bm{B}^{\prime}):={\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{B}}\sigma({\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{B}}\bm{B}^{\prime});" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>𝑩</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝑩</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{B}=g_{\bm{B}}(\bm{B}^{\prime}):={\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{B}}\sigma({\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{B}}\bm{B}^{\prime});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{A}=g_{\bm{A}}(\bm{A}^{\prime}):={\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{A}}\sigma({\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{A}}\bm{A}^{\prime})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑨</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>𝑨</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝑨</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{A}=g_{\bm{A}}(\bm{A}^{\prime}):={\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{A}}\sigma({\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{A}}\bm{A}^{\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}^{\prime},\bm{B}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}^{\prime},\bm{B}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable embedding, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\bm{B}},g_{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑩</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑨</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\bm{B}},g_{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> are two-layer feedforward networks with the activation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. In our experiments, the inputs <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}^{\prime},\bm{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}^{\prime},\bm{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the first layers <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{A}},{\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝑨</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝑩</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{A}},{\bm{W}}_{1}^{\bm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> are shared between query and value projections in each layer, while <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{A}},{\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>𝑨</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>𝑩</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{A}},{\bm{W}}_{2}^{\bm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct to emit head-specific adaptation matrices. To ensure parameter efficiency and facilitate knowledge sharing across attention heads, this hypernetwork is shared among attention heads in each layer. Bringing these components together, we have the following DoRAN updates, whose demonstration can be found in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{W}=m\frac{\bm{W}_{0}+g_{\bm{B}}(\bm{B}^{\prime})g_{\bm{A}}(\bm{A}^{\prime})}{\|\bm{W}_{0}+g_{\bm{B}}(\bm{B}^{\prime})g_{\bm{A}}(\bm{A}^{\prime})\|_{c}+\tau}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑨</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑩</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝑨</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{W}=m\frac{\bm{W}_{0}+g_{\bm{B}}(\bm{B}^{\prime})g_{\bm{A}}(\bm{A}^{\prime})}{\|\bm{W}_{0}+g_{\bm{B}}(\bm{B}^{\prime})g_{\bm{A}}(\bm{A}^{\prime})\|_{c}+\tau}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stabilization–Hypernetwork Synergy in DoRAN.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that the stabilization noise and the hypernetworks play complementary roles. As we will discuss in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Gradient Analysis ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, the learnable noise term <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> stabilizes optimization by ensuring that the gradients passed to the low-rank factors remain well-conditioned and preserve both orthogonal and parallel components of the signal. On the other hand, the hypernetwork then ensures that these gradients are not used to produce arbitrary low-rank updates, but rather to generate structured and consistent factors. In particular, the parameter sharing mechanism acts as an implicit regularizer: rather than producing arbitrary and potentially redundant low-rank solutions, the hypernetwork encourages consistency and reuse of common structure across layers, which has been shown in prior work to improve generalization of learned weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Gradient Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we examine the gradient properties of DoRAN to further highlight its theoretical benefits. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\prime}=\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\prime}=\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{B}\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(\bm{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(\bm{W})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the loss and define <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}=\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}=\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the upstream gradient. By the chain rule, the total gradient with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be given by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\bm{W}^{\prime}}=m\Bigg[\frac{{\bm{G}}}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau}-\frac{\left&lt;{\bm{G}},\bm{W}^{\prime}\right&gt;}{(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)^{2}}\frac{\bm{W}^{\prime}}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}}\Bigg]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>𝑮</mi><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\bm{W}^{\prime}}=m\Bigg[\frac{{\bm{G}}}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau}-\frac{\left&lt;{\bm{G}},\bm{W}^{\prime}\right&gt;}{(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)^{2}}\frac{\bm{W}^{\prime}}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}}\Bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Writing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the sum of its component orthogonal to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}_{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}_{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>) and its projection onto <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{proj}_{\bm{W}^{\prime}}({\bm{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>proj</mi><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{proj}_{\bm{W}^{\prime}}({\bm{G}})</annotation></semantics></math>), we obtain the equivalent decomposition:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\bm{W}^{\prime}}=\frac{m}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau}{\bm{G}}_{\perp}+\frac{m\tau}{(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)^{2}}\mathrm{proj}_{\bm{W}^{\prime}}({\bm{G}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>proj</mi><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\bm{W}^{\prime}}=\frac{m}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau}{\bm{G}}_{\perp}+\frac{m\tau}{(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)^{2}}\mathrm{proj}_{\bm{W}^{\prime}}({\bm{G}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This formulation reveals that the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">orthogonal component</em> of the gradient, which drives updates in the direction subspace, is scaled by a factor of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m/(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m/(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel component</em> (which controls norm changes) is preserved with a damping factor <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau/(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau/(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the parallel gradient <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{proj}_{{\bm{W}}^{\prime}}({\bm{G}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>proj</mi><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{proj}_{{\bm{W}}^{\prime}}({\bm{G}})</annotation></semantics></math> is not completely discarded (as in DoRA), hence the magnitude information is adaptively preserved. Therefore, in the limit <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the parallel component vanishes, recovering the DoRA update rule where only the direction of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized. Conversely, as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the update reduces to a rescaled linear adaptation <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}\approx(m/\tau)\bm{W}^{\prime}=(m/\tau)(\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{A}\bm{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}\approx(m/\tau)\bm{W}^{\prime}=(m/\tau)(\bm{W}_{0}+\bm{A}\bm{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, which resembles LoRA.
The gradients with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> are given by:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial m}=\left&lt;{\bm{G}},\frac{\bm{W}^{\prime}}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau}\right&gt;," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial m}=\left&lt;{\bm{G}},\frac{\bm{W}^{\prime}}{\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau}\right&gt;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\tau}=-m\left&lt;{\bm{G}},\frac{\bm{W}^{\prime}}{(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)^{2}}\right&gt;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial{\mathcal{L}}}{\partial\tau}=-m\left&lt;{\bm{G}},\frac{\bm{W}^{\prime}}{(\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau)^{2}}\right&gt;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">This equation reveals that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> governs global scaling, while <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> adaptively interpolates between strict normalization and linear scaling. To sum up, relative to DoRA and LoRA, the adaptive <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> of DoRAN provides the following effects:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stability:</span> The denominator <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c}+\tau</annotation></semantics></math> avoids the singularity at small norms, providing bounded gradients, which leads to more stable updates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Magnitude learning via <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> Unlike DoRA, the parallel component is partially retained, allowing <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A},\bm{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A},\bm{B}</annotation></semantics></math> to co-adapt both direction and norm.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Continuum of behaviors:</span> By adjusting <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the method interpolates smoothly between DoRA (<math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>) and LoRA-like scaling <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau\gg\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≫</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau\gg\|\bm{W}^{\prime}\|_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> enables the model to discover the most effective balance between direction learning and stable norm control.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the theoretical explanation of the advantages of the reparameterization technique implemented in DoRAN, through its connection to Mixture of Experts (MoE).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">DoRA meets MoE.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent works have shown that single-headed self-attention can be reinterpreted as a Mixture of Experts (MoE) model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, where each head or projection serves as an expert and the attention mechanism acts as the gating function. Building on this view, applying DoRA to an attention head can also be reformulated as an MoE model. Specifically, the attention head before and after applying DoRA can be expressed as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{\text{pre}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>head</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{\text{pre}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{softmax}\left({\bm{X}}\bm{W}_{Q}\bm{W}_{K}^{\top}{\bm{X}}^{\top}/\sqrt{d_{h}}\right){\bm{X}}\bm{W}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{h}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{softmax}\left({\bm{X}}\bm{W}_{Q}\bm{W}_{K}^{\top}{\bm{X}}^{\top}/\sqrt{d_{h}}\right){\bm{X}}\bm{W}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{h}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{\text{post}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>head</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{\text{post}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{softmax}\left({\bm{X}}m_{Q}\frac{\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q}}{\|\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q}\|\sqrt{d_{h}}}\bm{W}_{K}^{\top}{\bm{X}}^{\top}\right){\bm{X}}m_{V}\frac{\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V}}{\|\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V}\|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{softmax}\left({\bm{X}}m_{Q}\frac{\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q}}{\|\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q}\|\sqrt{d_{h}}}\bm{W}_{K}^{\top}{\bm{X}}^{\top}\right){\bm{X}}m_{V}\frac{\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V}}{\|\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V}\|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the softmax is applied to each row of the matrix inside. Therefore, the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{head}_{\text{post}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>head</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{head}_{\text{post}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{\text{post},i}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>head</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>post</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{head}_{\text{post},i}=\sum_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\mathrm{softmax}\left(\biggr\{\widetilde{\bm{X}}{\bm{E}}_{i}^{\top}\frac{m_{Q}(\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q})}{\|\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q}\|\sqrt{d_{h}}}\bm{W}_{K}^{\top}{\bm{E}}_{k}\widetilde{\bm{X}}\biggr\}_{k=1}^{N}\right)_{j}}_{\text{gating score for $j$-th expert }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝑬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>𝑾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝑬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>gating score for </mtext><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mtext>-th expert</mtext></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\mathrm{softmax}\left(\biggr\{\widetilde{\bm{X}}{\bm{E}}_{i}^{\top}\frac{m_{Q}(\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q})}{\|\bm{W}_{Q}+\bm{B}_{Q}\bm{A}_{Q}\|\sqrt{d_{h}}}\bm{W}_{K}^{\top}{\bm{E}}_{k}\widetilde{\bm{X}}\biggr\}_{k=1}^{N}\right)_{j}}_{\text{gating score for $j$-th expert }}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{m_{V}\frac{(\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V})^{\top}}{\|\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V}\|}{\bm{E}}_{j}\widetilde{\bm{X}}}_{\text{$j$-th expert }}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mtext>-th expert </mtext></mrow></munder><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{m_{V}\frac{(\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V})^{\top}}{\|\bm{W}_{V}+\bm{B}_{V}\bm{A}_{V}\|}{\bm{E}}_{j}\widetilde{\bm{X}}}_{\text{$j$-th expert }},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where we define <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{X}}:=\mathrm{Vec}(\bm{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{Nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{X}}:=\mathrm{Vec}(\bm{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{Nd}</annotation></semantics></math> and the matrix <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{E}}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times Nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{E}}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times Nd}</annotation></semantics></math> that extracts the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th token of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{E}}_{j}\widetilde{\bm{X}}=\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{E}}_{j}\widetilde{\bm{X}}=\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the attention head can be expressed as an MoE with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> experts and we will leverage this connection to analyze the properties of DoRA and DoRAN.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem setup.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{X}_{1},\bm{Y}_{1}),\ldots,(\bm{X}_{n},\bm{Y}_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒀</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒀</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{X}_{1},\bm{Y}_{1}),\ldots,(\bm{X}_{n},\bm{Y}_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be i.i.d. samples generated from the following regression model:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{Y}_{i}=f_{G_{*}}(\bm{X}_{i})+\varepsilon_{i},\quad i=1,2,\ldots,n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{Y}_{i}=f_{G_{*}}(\bm{X}_{i})+\varepsilon_{i},\quad i=1,2,\ldots,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We assume that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}_{1},\bm{X}_{2},\ldots,\bm{X}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}_{1},\bm{X}_{2},\ldots,\bm{X}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. samples from some probability distribution <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> with bounded support; <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{1},\varepsilon_{2},\ldots,\varepsilon_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{1},\varepsilon_{2},\ldots,\varepsilon_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent Gaussian noise with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\varepsilon_{i}|\bm{X}_{i})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\varepsilon_{i}|\bm{X}_{i})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(\varepsilon_{i}|\bm{X}_{i})=\sigma^{2}I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(\varepsilon_{i}|\bm{X}_{i})=\sigma^{2}I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The regression function <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{G_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{G_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the form of an MoE model with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> unknown experts,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{G_{*}}(\bm{X}):=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\mathrm{softmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>softmax</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{G_{*}}(\bm{X}):=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\mathrm{softmax}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\biggr\{\bm{X}^{\top}m_{Q,k}\frac{\bm{C}_{Q}+\bm{B}^{*}_{Q,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{Q,k}}{\|\bm{C}_{Q}+\bm{B}^{*}_{Q,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{Q,k}\|}\bm{C}_{K}\bm{X}+c^{*}_{k}\biggr\}_{k=1}^{L}\right)_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">{</mo></mrow><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\biggr\{\bm{X}^{\top}m_{Q,k}\frac{\bm{C}_{Q}+\bm{B}^{*}_{Q,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{Q,k}}{\|\bm{C}_{Q}+\bm{B}^{*}_{Q,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{Q,k}\|}\bm{C}_{K}\bm{X}+c^{*}_{k}\biggr\}_{k=1}^{L}\right)_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(m_{V,j}\frac{\bm{C}_{V}+\bm{B}^{*}_{V,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{V,j}}{\|\bm{C}_{V}+\bm{B}^{*}_{V,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{V,j}\|}\right)\bm{X}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(m_{V,j}\frac{\bm{C}_{V}+\bm{B}^{*}_{V,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{V,j}}{\|\bm{C}_{V}+\bm{B}^{*}_{V,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{V,j}\|}\right)\bm{X},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{*}=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\exp(c_{j}^{*})\delta_{({\bm{B}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{V,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}^{*})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{*}=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\exp(c_{j}^{*})\delta_{({\bm{B}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{V,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}^{*})}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the mixing measure, which is a combination of Dirac measures <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, associated with the true unknown parameters <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{V,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}^{*})_{j=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{V,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}^{*})_{j=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> in the compact parameter space <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}\times\mathbb{R}^{r\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}\times\mathbb{R}^{r\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}\times\mathbb{R}^{r\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}\times\mathbb{R}^{r\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pre-trained matrices <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{Q},\bm{C}_{K},\bm{C}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{Q},\bm{C}_{K},\bm{C}_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed and given (we changed the notation from matrix <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid confusion in subsequent analysis). To facilitate the analysis, we also assume that the magnitude parameters <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{m_{Q,j},m_{V,j}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{m_{Q,j},m_{V,j}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive and known.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the regression function in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Problem setup. ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) resembles the adaptation strategy of DoRA, with different low-rank matrices added to the query and value matrices of the pre-trained model. We prove that this mechanism causes suboptimal sample complexity in estimating the unknown low-rank matrices <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{B}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{V,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{B}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j}^{*},{\bm{B}}_{V,j}^{*},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Non-shared structure causes suboptimal sample complexity ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). We refer to this as the non-shared structure. On the other hand, we employ the sharing technique in DoRAN, with added noise to the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norms of the query and value matrices. We prove that these adjustments improve sample efficiency compared to the original technique (see Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Shared-structure with added noise ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Non-shared structure causes suboptimal sample complexity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We proceed to analyze the convergence rate of the low-rank matrices under the non-shared structure of the regression function in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Problem setup. ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). To study the convergence behavior, it is natural to approach the problem from the perspective of estimating the ground-truth mixing measure <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this purpose, we adopt the least-squares method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\textnormal{arg}\min_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta)}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Big({\bm{Y}}_{i}-f_{G}(\bm{X}_{i})\Big)^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>arg</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\textnormal{arg}\min_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta)}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Big({\bm{Y}}_{i}-f_{G}(\bm{X}_{i})\Big)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we denote by <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta):=\{G=\sum_{j=1}^{\ell}\exp(c_{j})\delta_{{\bm{B}}_{Q,j},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j},{\bm{B}}_{V,j},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}}:1\leq\ell\leq L^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta):=\{G=\sum_{j=1}^{\ell}\exp(c_{j})\delta_{{\bm{B}}_{Q,j},{\bm{A}}_{Q,j},{\bm{B}}_{V,j},{\bm{A}}_{V,j}}:1\leq\ell\leq L^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of all mixing measures with at most <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> atoms. Since the true number of experts <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is typically unknown, we assume that the number of fitted experts <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> chosen such that it is larger than <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. To determine the convergence rates of the above estimator <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the following loss function, which is constructed based on the Voronoi cells concept from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Voronoi loss.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a mixing measure <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}&gt;L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}&gt;L</annotation></semantics></math> atoms, the Voronoi cell set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{V}_{j}\equiv\mathcal{V}_{j}(G):j\in[L]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{V}_{j}\equiv\mathcal{V}_{j}(G):j\in[L]\}</annotation></semantics></math>
is generated by the atoms of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{j}:=\{i\in[L^{\prime}]:\|{\bm{H}}_{i}-{\bm{H}}^{*}_{j}\|\leq\|{\bm{H}}_{i}-{\bm{H}}^{*}_{\ell}\|,\forall\ell\neq j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{j}:=\{i\in[L^{\prime}]:\|{\bm{H}}_{i}-{\bm{H}}^{*}_{j}\|\leq\|{\bm{H}}_{i}-{\bm{H}}^{*}_{\ell}\|,\forall\ell\neq j\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}:=({\bm{B}}_{Q},{\bm{A}}_{Q},{\bm{B}}_{V},{\bm{A}}_{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}:=({\bm{B}}_{Q},{\bm{A}}_{Q},{\bm{B}}_{V},{\bm{A}}_{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we define the Voronoi loss for the non-shared structure,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{1,r}(G,G_{*}):=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\Big|\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})-\exp(c^{*}_{j})\Big|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{1,r}(G,G_{*}):=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\Big|\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})-\exp(c^{*}_{j})\Big|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle+\sum_{j=1}^{L}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})(\|\Delta{\bm{B}}_{Q,ij}\|^{r}+\|\Delta{\bm{A}}_{Q,ij}\|^{r}+\|\Delta{\bm{B}}_{V,ij}\|^{r}+\|\Delta{\bm{A}}_{V,ij}\|^{r})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\sum_{j=1}^{L}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})(\|\Delta{\bm{B}}_{Q,ij}\|^{r}+\|\Delta{\bm{A}}_{Q,ij}\|^{r}+\|\Delta{\bm{B}}_{V,ij}\|^{r}+\|\Delta{\bm{A}}_{V,ij}\|^{r}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we use the notation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\bm{A}}_{ij}:={\bm{A}}_{i}-{\bm{A}}^{*}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\bm{A}}_{ij}:={\bm{A}}_{i}-{\bm{A}}^{*}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for a matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> to quantify the estimation error. It is evident that once the order of the loss value is determined, the convergence rate of the estimators can be readily inferred. The following theorem presents the minimax rate of the Voronoi loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the setting of non-shared structure defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Problem setup. ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and Eq. (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Problem setup. ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the following minimax lower bound of estimating <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.1 Non-shared structure causes suboptimal sample complexity ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) holds for any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{G}_{n}\in\mathcal{G}_{L^{{}^{\prime}}}}\sup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta)\setminus\mathcal{G}_{L-1}(\Theta)}\mathbb{E}_{f_{G}}[\mathcal{D}_{1,r}(\widehat{G}_{n},{G})]\gtrsim\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{G}_{n}\in\mathcal{G}_{L^{{}^{\prime}}}}\sup_{G\in\mathcal{G}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta)\setminus\mathcal{G}_{L-1}(\Theta)}\mathbb{E}_{f_{G}}[\mathcal{D}_{1,r}(\widehat{G}_{n},{G})]\gtrsim\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{f_{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{f_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the expectation taken w.r.t. the product measure <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{n}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{n}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Voronoi loss. ‣ 4.1 Non-shared structure causes suboptimal sample complexity ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>. Since the minimax lower bound above holds for any natural number <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the order of the estimation error in the Voronoi loss, it follows that the convergence rates of the low-rank matrix estimators <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bm{B}}^{n}_{Q,j},{\bm{A}}^{n}_{Q,j},{\bm{B}}^{n}_{V,j},{\bm{A}}^{n}_{Q,j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bm{B}}^{n}_{Q,j},{\bm{A}}^{n}_{Q,j},{\bm{B}}^{n}_{V,j},{\bm{A}}^{n}_{Q,j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> would be slower than any polynomial rates <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{P}(n^{-1/2r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{P}(n^{-1/2r})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the convergence rate could become as slow as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{P}(1/\log^{a}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{P}(1/\log^{a}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence, we need an exponential sample size of order <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\exp(\epsilon^{-1/a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\exp(\epsilon^{-1/a})</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the estimation error <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. This observation underscores the limited sample efficiency of the non-sharing structure. At a high level, the main technical obstacle to the above convergence rate arises from the fact that normalizing the adapted matrix induces an interaction among the parameters through the following partial differential equation (PDE):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\langle\bm{C}_{Q}+{\bm{B}}{\bm{A}},\frac{\partial}{\partial({\bm{B}}{\bm{A}})}\exp\left(\bm{X}^{\top}m_{Q}\frac{\bm{C}_{Q}+{\bm{B}}{\bm{A}}}{\|\bm{C}_{Q}+{\bm{B}}{\bm{A}}\|}\bm{C}_{K}\bm{X}\right)\right\rangle=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\langle\bm{C}_{Q}+{\bm{B}}{\bm{A}},\frac{\partial}{\partial({\bm{B}}{\bm{A}})}\exp\left(\bm{X}^{\top}m_{Q}\frac{\bm{C}_{Q}+{\bm{B}}{\bm{A}}}{\|\bm{C}_{Q}+{\bm{B}}{\bm{A}}\|}\bm{C}_{K}\bm{X}\right)\right\rangle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This PDE causes the linear dependence of the terms in the Taylor expansion of the density discrepancy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\widehat{G}_{n}}(\bm{X})-f_{G_{*}}(\bm{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\widehat{G}_{n}}(\bm{X})-f_{G_{*}}(\bm{X})</annotation></semantics></math> that affect the convergence of expert estimation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Shared-structure with added noise</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we consider the following non-linear reparameterization that shares the parameters within the low-rank adapting matrices:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{A}}_{Q}={\bm{A}}_{V}=\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}_{1}\bm{A}),\quad{\bm{B}}_{Q}={\bm{B}}_{V}=\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}_{2}\bm{B})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{A}}_{Q}={\bm{A}}_{V}=\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}_{1}\bm{A}),\quad{\bm{B}}_{Q}={\bm{B}}_{V}=\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}_{2}\bm{B}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d},{\bm{B}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m^{\prime}\times r},\bm{W}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m},\bm{W}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d},{\bm{B}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m^{\prime}\times r},\bm{W}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m},\bm{W}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable matrices with given dimension <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are some non-linear activation functions. Moreover, we perturb the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm components of the query and value matrices by adding a small noise term in the denominator. In addition to the aforementioned benefits of the added noise, we note that it also helps avoid the interaction in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In Voronoi loss. ‣ 4.1 Non-shared structure causes suboptimal sample complexity ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). With these adjustments, the ground-truth regression function is:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\widetilde{G}_{*}}(\bm{X}):=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\mathrm{softmax}\left(\biggr\{\bm{X}^{\top}m_{Q,k}\frac{\bm{C}_{Q}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,k}\bm{B}^{*}_{k})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{k})}{\|\bm{C}_{Q}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,k}\bm{B}^{*}_{k})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{k})\|+\tau_{Q}}\bm{C}_{K}\bm{X}+c^{*}_{k}\biggr\}_{k=1}^{L}\right)_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">{</mo></mrow><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\widetilde{G}_{*}}(\bm{X}):=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\mathrm{softmax}\left(\biggr\{\bm{X}^{\top}m_{Q,k}\frac{\bm{C}_{Q}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,k}\bm{B}^{*}_{k})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{k})}{\|\bm{C}_{Q}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,k}\bm{B}^{*}_{k})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,k}\bm{A}^{*}_{k})\|+\tau_{Q}}\bm{C}_{K}\bm{X}+c^{*}_{k}\biggr\}_{k=1}^{L}\right)_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip 113.81102pt\times\left(m_{V,j}\frac{\bm{C}_{V}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}\bm{B}^{*}_{j})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{j})}{\|\bm{C}_{V}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}\bm{B}^{*}_{j})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{j})\|+\tau_{V}}\right)\bm{X}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="11.602em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip 113.81102pt\times\left(m_{V,j}\frac{\bm{C}_{V}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}\bm{B}^{*}_{j})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{j})}{\|\bm{C}_{V}+\sigma_{2}(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}\bm{B}^{*}_{j})\sigma_{1}(\bm{W}^{*}_{1,j}\bm{A}^{*}_{j})\|+\tau_{V}}\right)\bm{X},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">here the ground-truth mixing measure is <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}_{*}:=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\exp(c^{*}_{j})\delta_{(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}{\bm{B}}_{j}^{*},\bm{W}^{*}_{1,j}{\bm{A}}_{j}^{*})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}_{*}:=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\exp(c^{*}_{j})\delta_{(\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}{\bm{B}}_{j}^{*},\bm{W}^{*}_{1,j}{\bm{A}}_{j}^{*})}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we consider the least-square method for our estimator, i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}_{n}\in\textnormal{arg}\min_{\widetilde{G}\in\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta)}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Big({\bm{Y}}_{i}-f_{\widetilde{G}}(\bm{X}_{i})\Big)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>arg</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}_{n}\in\textnormal{arg}\min_{\widetilde{G}\in\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta)}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Big({\bm{Y}}_{i}-f_{\widetilde{G}}(\bm{X}_{i})\Big)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta):=\{G=\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\exp(c_{i})\delta_{(\bm{W}_{2,i}{\bm{B}}_{i},\bm{W}_{1,i}{\bm{A}}_{i})}:1\leq\ell\leq L^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{G}}_{L^{\prime}}(\Theta):=\{G=\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\exp(c_{i})\delta_{(\bm{W}_{2,i}{\bm{B}}_{i},\bm{W}_{1,i}{\bm{A}}_{i})}:1\leq\ell\leq L^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Voronoi loss function in this setting is constructed as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{2}(\widetilde{G},\widetilde{G}_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{2}(\widetilde{G},\widetilde{G}_{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\Big|\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})-\exp(c_{j}^{*})\Big|+\sum_{j\in[L]:|\mathcal{V}_{j}|=1}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})(\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{2}\bm{B})_{ij}\|+\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{1}\bm{A})_{ij}\|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\Big|\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})-\exp(c_{j}^{*})\Big|+\sum_{j\in[L]:|\mathcal{V}_{j}|=1}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})(\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{2}\bm{B})_{ij}\|+\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{1}\bm{A})_{ij}\|)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle+\sum_{j\in[L]:|\mathcal{V}_{j}|&gt;1}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})(\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{2}\bm{B})_{ij}\|^{2}+\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{1}\bm{A})_{ij}\|^{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\sum_{j\in[L]:|\mathcal{V}_{j}|&gt;1}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}_{j}}\exp(c_{i})(\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{2}\bm{B})_{ij}\|^{2}+\|\Delta(\bm{W}_{1}\bm{A})_{ij}\|^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where we denote, for example, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\bm{W}_{2}{\bm{B}})_{ij}:=\bm{W}_{2,i}{\bm{B}}_{i}-\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}{\bm{B}}^{*}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\bm{W}_{2}{\bm{B}})_{ij}:=\bm{W}_{2,i}{\bm{B}}_{i}-\bm{W}^{*}_{2,j}{\bm{B}}^{*}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Before presenting the main theorem, we introduce some mild assumptions on the activation functions <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the space constraint, we defer these assumptions until the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Shared-structure with added noise ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in Appendix  <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that the activation functions <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> meet the assumptions specified in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>. Then, the estimator <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the true mixing measure <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> at the following rate: <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{2}(\widetilde{G}_{n},\widetilde{G}_{*})=\mathcal{O}_{P}(\sqrt{\log(n)/n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{2}(\widetilde{G}_{n},\widetilde{G}_{*})=\mathcal{O}_{P}(\sqrt{\log(n)/n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Shared-structure with added noise ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.
Based on the construction of the loss <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{2}(\widetilde{G},\widetilde{G}_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{2}(\widetilde{G},\widetilde{G}_{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Shared-structure with added noise ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> suggests that the convergence rate of the estimators <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{2,j}{\bm{B}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{2,j}{\bm{B}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{1,j}{\bm{A}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{1,j}{\bm{A}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to the true matrices <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{2,j}^{*}{\bm{B}}_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{2,j}^{*}{\bm{B}}_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{1,j}^{*}{\bm{A}}_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{1,j}^{*}{\bm{A}}_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are ranging from order <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{P}([\log(n)/n]^{\frac{1}{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{P}([\log(n)/n]^{\frac{1}{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>, if the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th Voronoi cell <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> has one element, to order <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{P}([\log(n)/n]^{\frac{1}{4}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{P}([\log(n)/n]^{\frac{1}{4}})</annotation></semantics></math>, if the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th Voronoi cell <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> has more than one element. In both cases, we only need the sample size with the order at most <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{-4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{-4})</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve the desired estimation error <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, compared with the required sample size of order <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\exp(\epsilon^{-1/a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\exp(\epsilon^{-1/a}))</annotation></semantics></math> of the non-shared structure analyzed in the previous section.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Settings.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents experimental results evaluating the effectiveness of our proposed methods: <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau-</annotation></semantics></math>DoRA, which refers to DoRA with our stabilization term <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, and DoRAN. We consider two tasks: Image Classification on VTAB-1K and FGVC, and Commonsense Reasoning. We compare our method against several PEFT methods, including <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LoRA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prefix Tuning</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Parallel Adapter</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DoRA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. We also conduct a Sample Efficiency task comparing DoRAN and DoRA on the Commonsense Reasoning task. For consistency with the theoretical setting, we fine-tune the query and value projection matrices of each attention layer. Additionally, we present an extended variant of DoRAN that applies low-rank matrices to the proj_up and proj_down matrices in both LLaMA-7B and LLaMA-13B, as described in Ablation <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Ablation on Low-Rank Matrices in Query, Value, Up, and Down Projections ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>. Full hyperparameter details are in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Implementation Details ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image Classification.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider the vision domain, where we aim to fine-tune the ViT-B/16 variant of the Vision Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, which was fine-tuned on the ImageNet-21K dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. This experiment consists of two benchmarks. The first is the Visual Task Adaptation Benchmark (VTAB-1K), a transfer-learning benchmark that evaluates vision models on 19 image classification tasks across three domains—Natural, Specialized, and Structured—using only 1000 labeled examples per task. Per-domain results are reported in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Figure 2 ‣ Image Classification. ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with detailed results in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Detail of results on VTAB-1K datasets ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>. When incorporating stabilization into DoRA (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau-</annotation></semantics></math>DoRA), as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 DoRAN: DoRA with Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the resulting <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau-</annotation></semantics></math>DoRA improves overall performance by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> compared to the original DoRA with nearly no additional computational overhead, suggesting the practical benefits of including this stabilization term. Moreover, with hypernetwork-based parameter generation, our method DoRAN significantly outperforms all baselines, notably improving over DoRA by 1.1% on Natural, 1.6% on Specialized, and 1.8% on Structured tasks. While hypernetworks inevitably introduce additional parameters, sharing half of the architecture across attention heads and query/value projections greatly reduces this overhead. As a result, DoRAN requires only <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.09\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.09</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.09\%</annotation></semantics></math> more trainable parameters relative to total parameters of ViT compared to DoRA, yet achieves significant performance gains.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
