</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Commonsense Reasoning.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having shown the effectiveness of both <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau-</annotation></semantics></math>DoRA and DoRAN on vision tasks, we now evaluate them on language tasks using the commonsense reasoning benchmark, which includes eight sub-tasks. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we merge all datasets into a 150k training set and test on LLaMA-7B and 13B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Commonsense Reasoning. ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau-</annotation></semantics></math>DoRA outperforms most datasets on LLaMA-7B and improves DoRA by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> (7B) and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> (13B), demonstrating the benefit of added noise. DoRAN, combining noise with hypernetwork-based reparameterization, achieves the best average accuracy on both scales: 77.01% on 7B (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> over DoRA, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx+3.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx+3.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> over LoRA) and 80.76% on 13B (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> over DoRA). On LLaMA-7B, it performs especially well on HellaSwag (83.45%), ARC-c (65.02%), and OBQA (79.6%, best in block). Overall, DoRAN consistently improves commonsense reasoning, with gains persisting on harder benchmarks (e.g., ARC-c), while adding only negligible parameters compared to DoRA and LoRA.</p>
</div>
