<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Ablation on Low-Rank Matrices in Query, Value, Up, and Down Projections</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond applying low-rank matrices to the query and value matrices in each layer, we also explore whether our DoRA-based design can generalize when extended to additional modules. In this setting, we also apply low-rank matrices to the proj_up and proj_down matrices, while maintaining our proposed design for the query and value matrices and using the standard low-rank formulation for the proj_up and proj_down. As in Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.4 Ablation on Low-Rank Matrices in Query, Value, Up, and Down Projections ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ DoRAN: Stabilizing Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation via Noise Injection and Auxiliary Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, DoRAN achieves the best performance in both LLaMA-7B and LLaMA-13B settings, surpassing LoRA and DoRA by an average of 1.2% and 1.0% on the LLaMA-7B setting, and by 1.9% and 0.9% on the LLaMA-13B setting, respectively. These results indicate that our method, originally designed for query and value matrices in multi-head attention, remains effective even when low-rank matrices are applied more broadly across the model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
